   "doc": "generic hash map.\n\n`Map<K, V>` is keyed off a `KeyOps<K>` ops struct carrying the\nhash + equality function pointers - the caller supplies the\nbehavior at construction time. This pattern is the language's\nstand-in for a `Hashable` trait with `Self`-typed `eq`, which yoop\ndoes not have. Two function-pointer-field lifts make it work\nend-to-end: func-decl to FPT coercion at assignment, and indirect\ncall through `ops.hash(k)` / `ops.eq(a, b)`.\n\nImplementation: open-addressing with linear probing.\n  * Backing arrays: `keys: K[]`, `values: V[]`, `states: uint8[]`.\n    `states[i]` is one of `STATE_EMPTY` / `STATE_OCCUPIED` /\n    `STATE_TOMBSTONE`. Lookups probe until `EMPTY`; tombstones don't\n    stop the probe so removals don't break chains.\n  * Load factor: grow at 75% (occupied + tombstones) of cap.\n  * Hash: caller's `ops.hash`; bucket index = `hash & (cap - 1)`,\n    so `cap` is always a power of two. `mapNew` rounds up\n    `initial_cap` to the next power of two with a floor of 8.\n  * Disposable: owns three heap allocations; one `dispose` frees\n    them all. Carries `propagates<disposable>` like `Vec<T>`.\n\nPre-built ops for common key types ship below: `stringKeyOps()`\nand `int32KeyOps()`. Users with custom key types provide their\nown `KeyOps<K>` by naming a pair of free-function decls.",
