   "doc": "allocators and the ambient allocation context.\n\nAn `Allocator` is a small value: a record of function pointers plus an\nopaque data pointer (the same shape as `KeyOps`, and the same shape Jai and\nOdin use). The \"current allocator\" is ambient, held per-thread by the\nruntime; `pushAllocator` / `popAllocator` swap it for a region of code, and\nthe standard library allocates from whatever is current.\n\nThis file holds the malloc allocator, a bump arena, and the manual\npush/pop context. The `arena` scope keyword, routing the std containers\nthrough the context, and the temp allocator build on top of this. As with\nanything under std/, value imports here use the namespace form:\n\n    import * as mem from \"std/core/alloc.yoop\";\n    let a: mem.Arena = mem.arenaNew(64 * 1024);\n    let prev: mem.Allocator = mem.pushAllocator(mem.arenaAllocator(a));\n    let p: unsafe_ptr<void> = mem.allocBytes(128, 8);\n    mem.popAllocator(prev);\n    mem.arenaDestroy(a);",
