   "doc": "TLS over a TcpStream, via OpenSSL.\n\nClient-side only. What it gives you:\n\n    import * as tls from \"std/tls\";\n\n    let cfg: tls.TlsConfig = tls.tlsConfigNew();\n    disposable s: tls.TlsStream = await tls.tlsConnect(\"example.com\", 443, ref cfg)?;\n    // s implements Readable + Writable, so anything taking a Reader/Writer\n    // takes it - including all of std/http.\n\nCertificate verification and hostname checking are ON. Turning them off is\nper-connection, explicit, and named so it cannot be typed by accident.\n\nThis is a DIRECTORY MODULE of its own rather than a file inside std/net, and\nthat is load-bearing: link flags are collected program-wide, so an\n`extern from library \"ssl\"` inside std/net would make every program that\nopens a socket link OpenSSL. Importing std/tls is what pulls the dependency\nin - and what compiles runtime/yoop_tls.c into the program.\n\nDesign, and why the ciphertext moves through yoop rather than through\nOpenSSL's own socket I/O: runtime/yoop_tls.c.",
