     "doc": "How a connection should be verified.\n\nThe defaults are the safe ones and the unsafe knob is spelled out in full.\nA boolean named `verify` defaulting to false is how a TLS client ends up\ntrusting anything; this one takes a sentence to type.",
     "shape": "export type TlsConfig {\n    // Trust roots. Empty means OpenSSL's compiled-in default paths, which is\n    // right on Linux and on a normal macOS OpenSSL install, and finds nothing\n    // on Windows, which has no bridge to the system certificate store.\n    // Point it at a PEM bundle to pin, or to trust a private CA.\n    caFile: string,\n\n    // Turns off BOTH chain verification and hostname checking. Named at\n    // length on purpose: this is the switch that makes TLS decorative.\n    dangerouslyDisableVerification: bool,\n\n    // The name to VERIFY against and to send as SNI, when that differs from\n    // the address dialled. Empty means \"use the host you connected to\", which\n    // is the usual case.\n    //\n    // The two genuinely come apart: dialling an IP while the certificate\n    // carries a DNS name, going through a proxy or a tunnel, or connecting to\n    // one node of a cluster that all present the service's name. Without this,\n    // the only way to reach those is to turn verification off entirely - which\n    // is how a knob meant for one narrow case ends up disabling everything.\n    serverName: string,\n}",
